# mod_pagespeed — Full Reference ({{PRODUCT_NAME}} {{CURRENT_LINE}})

> Self-hosted web performance optimization for Apache and nginx. {{LICENSE_CLAUSE_CAP}}. Built by a mod_pagespeed maintainer. {{PRODUCT_NAME}} {{CURRENT_LINE}} converges the mod_pagespeed lineage and the ModPageSpeed 2.0 engine into one product: a native web-server module with a separate optimizer worker.

{{PRODUCT_NAME}} {{CURRENT_LINE}} is a self-hosted product by We-Amp B.V., {{LICENSE_CLAUSE}}, that automatically optimizes web content. A native in-process module for Apache and nginx handles serving, and a separate optimizer worker — sharing a disk cache with the module — does the heavy optimization work outside the web server. Drop-in compatible with the classic module: same configuration directives, same filters. No application code changes, no URL rewrites, no build pipeline modifications required.

## Product Overview

ModPageSpeed 2.0 replaces the original mod_pagespeed RewriteDriver (2000+ LOC, 60+ filters) and uses the underlying PSOL optimization libraries directly. Optimization happens outside the request path in a separate worker process. The nginx module is a thin caching interceptor that classifies requests, serves cache hits via zero-copy mmap, and notifies the worker on misses.

### Heritage

mod_pagespeed is the open-source optimization core this product continues. Per BuiltWith ({{BUILTWITH_AS_OF}}), it powers {{BUILTWITH_SITES}} live sites today ({{BUILTWITH_HISTORICAL}} across its full history). These sites are not We-Amp customers — they are evidence the optimization core works at scale. {{PRODUCT_NAME}} {{CURRENT_LINE}} converges the two lines We-Amp shipped through 2026: the maintained continuation of the open-source codebase (mod_pagespeed {{V1_LINE}}, renumbered from {{V1_RENUMBERED_FROM}} — the forward-semver successor to the last upstream release, {{LAST_UPSTREAM_VERSION}}) and the ground-up ModPageSpeed 2.0 optimization engine, which continues as the optimizer worker.

## Image Optimization

- Automatic WebP and AVIF transcoding based on the client's Accept header
- Viewport-aware resizing: Mobile (480px), Tablet (768px), Desktop (original)
- Pixel density support: 1x and 2x+ (Retina) variants
- Save-Data support: reduced quality when the user opts for less data
- Proactive variant generation: a single image decode produces up to {{MAX_VARIANTS}} variants per image — {{RASTER_VARIANTS}} raster (3 formats x 3 viewports x 2 densities x 2 Save-Data states) plus 1 SVG for eligible images
- Lossless PNG reduction via OptiPNG
- Animated GIF to animated WebP conversion
- Quality settings: JPEG 85/60, WebP 75/50, AVIF 25/35 (normal/Save-Data)
- Learned Quality Prediction: Per-format ML models (LightGBM compiled to C) predict optimal encoder quality for target SSIMULACRA2. Flags: --no-learned-quality, --no-learned-quality-{jpeg,webp,avif}. Falls back to heuristic when disabled or prediction invalid.
- Content-aware quality classification: images classified as photo, screenshot, illustration, or noisy; compression adapted per class
- Image denoising: bilateral filter applied to noisy images before encoding (--denoise-threshold, --denoise-sigma-spatial, --denoise-sigma-range)
- Quality baselining: JPEG source quality detected from DQT tables; output capped at source quality + margin to prevent bloating low-quality sources
- SSIMULACRA2 perceptual verification: re-encodes with adjusted quality until target score is met (--target-ssimulacra2, --ssimulacra2-tolerance, --ssimulacra2-max-attempts)
- Safety limit: 50 MB max decoded pixel buffer (kMaxDecodedPixels)

## CSS Optimization

- Four-phase minification: comment stripping, whitespace collapsing, trailing semicolon removal, decimal optimization (0.5 to .5)
- Heuristic-based critical CSS extraction (works standalone, optional browser pipeline for Lighthouse validation)
- Async CSS loading: render-blocking stylesheets made non-blocking by switching them to rel="preload" as="style" and turning them back into stylesheets, with their real media, once loaded, driven by a CSP-safe external loader served at a content-hashed path under /pagespeed_static/ (e.g. /pagespeed_static/async_css.<hash>.js; the hash changes when the loader changes, so it is cached immutably). No inline onload, so it works under script-src 'self', with a <noscript> fallback for clients without JavaScript. The inlined critical CSS paints first; the full sheet applies once loaded. Activated whenever critical CSS is present. Disable: --no-async-css
- Critical selectors: universal/html/body/:root (always), first 25 DOM elements, semantic patterns (header, nav, hero, banner, masthead)
- Exclusion patterns: footer, lazy, defer, below-fold, @media print
- Critical CSS injected as a <style> tag before </head>
- Size-gated: only writes minified variant if actually smaller

## JavaScript Optimization

- Safe minification: whitespace and comment removal only
- No variable renaming, no AST transforms
- Script deferral: browser script analysis identifies scripts safe to defer; worker adds `defer` attribute automatically. Skips scripts with async, defer, or type="module". Disable: --no-script-deferral
- Size-gated: only writes minified variant if actually smaller

## HTML Optimization

- Critical CSS injection into HTML variants
- Async CSS loading: render-blocking stylesheets converted to non-blocking (rel="preload" as="style" swap + CSP-safe external loader), whenever critical CSS is present
- Script deferral: safe scripts identified by browser analysis receive `defer` attribute
- Early Hints (103): preload hints stored in cache, sent before origin responds
- Preload hints include stylesheets, LCP images (image: prefix), and third-party origins (preconnect: prefix)
- Lazy loading: automatic `loading="lazy"` on `<img>` and `<iframe>` tags, with the LCP candidate image (or first body image as fallback) receiving `fetchpriority="high"` instead
- Image dimensions: automatic `width`/`height` attribute injection on `<img>` tags from cached image headers (prevents CLS, no full decode required)
- Hot URL warmup: frequently-accessed URLs get proactive variant generation
- LCP image preload: detected LCP candidate receives `<link rel="preload" as="image">` hint
- Font preloading: font URLs from CSS/HTML injected as preload hints to prevent FOIT
- Preconnect injection: third-party origins get `<link rel="preconnect">` headers
- Speculation rules (experimental): opt-in prefetch via Speculation Rules API for hot URLs (--enable-speculation-rules)

## Variant-Aware Caching

### 32-Bit Capability Bitmask

```
Bits 0-1:  Image Format      00=Original, 01=WebP, 10=AVIF, 11=SVG
Bits 2-3:  Viewport Class    00=Mobile, 01=Tablet, 10=Desktop
Bit  4:    Pixel Density     0=1x, 1=2x+
Bit  5:    Save-Data         0=off, 1=on
Bits 6-7:  Transfer Encoding 00=Identity, 01=Gzip, 10=Brotli, 11=Reserved
```

Viewport pixel ranges: Mobile 0-479px, Tablet 480-1023px, Desktop 1024+px.

### Cache Key Format

Cache keys are SHA-256(URL, hostname). Multiple variants per URL stored as alternates, each identified by an AlternateId (low 8 bits of capability mask). Per-alternate metadata carries the full 32-bit mask, content type, flags, and origin content-type string.

### Best-Fit Fallback

On cache miss, the selector scores all stored alternates by mask similarity:
- Format match: +1000 points (Original fallback: +100)
- Viewport match: +80 points
- Encoding match: +60 exact, +5 identity fallback
- Density match: +40 points
- Save-Data match: +20 points
- Mismatched non-identity encoding: hard disqualification (score 0)

Original content always serves as reliable fallback.

### Cache Write Invariant

Nginx only writes the default-mask alternate (original content, mask 0x08). The worker writes all other alternates. No write contention between processes.

## Zero-Copy Serving

The Cyclone cache maps optimized resources directly into nginx output buffers using mmap. The cache hit path is: hash lookup, pointer assignment, send. No data copying, no per-request memory allocation for content. Sub-millisecond latency.

## Architecture

### Three Components

1. **Nginx Interceptor** — C++ nginx dynamic module. Classifies requests via Accept, Client Hints, Save-Data, and User-Agent headers. Composes cache keys. Serves hits via zero-copy mmap. Passes misses to origin. Sends fire-and-forget notifications to worker.

2. **Cyclone Cache** — Lock-free, memory-mapped disk cache with capability-based keys. Supports best-fit fallback. Memory-mapped directory for cross-process visibility. Both nginx and worker share the same cache file with enable_mmap_directory=true.

3. **Factory Worker** — Lightweight C++ worker using libuv for async I/O. Receives notifications via Unix socket. Runs HTML parsing, image transcoding, CSS/JS minification, and critical CSS extraction. Writes results to Cyclone cache.

### Cross-Process Communication

- Unix socket: fire-and-forget notifications from nginx to worker
- Shared cache file: mmap'd, visible to both processes immediately
- Health check socket: worker status and statistics
- Management socket: STATS, PURGE, METRICS, BROWSER-STATUS, and cache inspection

## Installation

### Docker (Recommended)

Three containers sharing a named volume:
1. Your origin server (any HTTP server)
2. ghcr.io/we-amp/pagespeed-worker:2.1.0 (factory worker)
3. ghcr.io/we-amp/pagespeed-nginx:2.1.0 (nginx with pagespeed module)

Shared volume at /shared contains the cache file and Unix socket.
Key: chmod 777 /shared, chmod 666 cache.vol for cross-process access.

### Nginx Module (Bare Metal)

1. Download ngx_pagespeed_module.so matching your nginx version
2. Download factory_worker binary
3. Create the cache directory (/var/cache/pagespeed-optimizer/v1/) owned by the worker's user and group, mode 3770 — setgid so the group is inherited, sticky so one group member cannot remove another's files. The packaged deb/rpm does this for you via tmpfiles.d.
4. Add load_module and pagespeed directives to nginx.conf, pointing pagespeed_cache_path at the cache stem inside that directory
5. Create a systemd service for factory_worker running as the unprivileged pagespeed user (User=/Group=pagespeed, RuntimeDirectory=pagespeed-optimizer). Every shared mode is set explicitly by the worker — sockets and volume 0660, shared config 0640 — so UMask is a backstop only, never the mechanism.
6. Add the nginx worker user to group pagespeed and restart nginx: that group membership is what lets it reach the cache and socket.

### Key Nginx Directives

- pagespeed on|off — enable/disable the module
- pagespeed_cache_path PATH — path to Cyclone cache volume file
- pagespeed_disallow PATTERN — skip optimization for matching URLs
- pagespeed_hot_threshold N — fallback-hit count before warmup (default: 5)
- pagespeed_force_refresh_html on|off — force revalidation on browser Ctrl+F5 for HTML (default: on)
- pagespeed_force_refresh on|off — force revalidation on browser Ctrl+F5 for all non-HTML types (default: off)

### Shared Configuration (pagespeed-shared.conf)

The worker writes a `pagespeed-shared.conf` file next to the cache file, mode 0640 (owner + group `pagespeed`). Nginx reads it automatically (polled every ~1s). Fields: socket_path, disable_html, cache_dir_generation. This eliminates the need for separate nginx directives for these settings.

### Key Worker Flags

- --socket PATH — Unix socket path (default: /run/pagespeed-optimizer/notify.sock)
- --cache-dir DIR — directory holding the cache volume, shared config and serve-stats (default: /var/cache/pagespeed-optimizer/v1)
- --cache-path PATH — expert override: full Cyclone cache volume file stem (derived from --cache-dir when unset)
- --cache-size BYTES — max cache size (default: 1073741824 = 1 GB)
- --disable-image — disable image optimization
- --disable-css — disable CSS optimization
- --disable-js — disable JS optimization
- --disable-html — disable HTML optimization
- --proactive-image-variants — enable multi-format proactive generation (default: off)
- --no-proactive-viewport-variants — skip viewport siblings
- --no-proactive-savedata-variants — skip Save-Data siblings
- --no-proactive-density-variants — skip density siblings
- --mobile-width PIXELS — mobile resize target (default: 480)
- --tablet-width PIXELS — tablet resize target (default: 768)

