## mod_pagespeed {{V1_LINE}}

mod_pagespeed {{V1_LINE}} is a native in-process optimization module for the broadest server matrix — Apache, nginx, and IIS GA, with Envoy experimental. It is the maintained continuation of the open-source mod_pagespeed project (originally developed at Google) and stays drop-in compatible with it: all existing pagespeed configuration directives, filters, and behavior carry over unchanged. Renumbered from {{V1_RENUMBERED_FROM}} (forward-semver successor to the last upstream release, {{LAST_UPSTREAM_VERSION}}). Developed by We-Amp B.V.

### Architecture

mod_pagespeed {{V1_LINE}} runs as an in-process module inside the web server. It intercepts HTTP responses, applies optimization filters to HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and images, then serves the optimized content. Optimized resources are cached in Cyclone Cache for subsequent requests.

Supported platforms (availability):
- Apache (drop-in replacement for the open-source module) — GA
- nginx (dynamic module) — GA. The `nginx-module-pagespeed` package is prebuilt and signed, with a .so pinned to each distro's stock nginx: {{NGINX_APT_MATRIX}}, {{NGINX_APT_ARCHES}}. On AlmaLinux 9 the nginx module targets stock nginx {{STOCK_NGINX_ALMA}} via the yum repository. Each build is exact-version-pinned to its distro's nginx, so running an nginx version we don't yet package needs a matching pinned build — contact us for one.
- IIS (native Windows Server module, replaces IISpeed) — GA
- Envoy (HTTP filter) — experimental

### Install ({{V1_LINE}})

One-time repository setup, then install per platform:

```
curl -fsSL https://packages.modpagespeed.com/install.sh | sudo sh
# Apache:
sudo apt install mod-pagespeed          # Debian / Ubuntu
sudo dnf install mod-pagespeed          # AlmaLinux 9
# nginx (prebuilt, signed; pinned to your distro's stock nginx):
sudo apt install nginx-module-pagespeed # Debian 11/12/13, Ubuntu 22.04/24.04 (amd64 + arm64)
sudo dnf install nginx-module-pagespeed # AlmaLinux 9 (stock nginx {{STOCK_NGINX_ALMA}})
```

IIS installs via the signed .msi for Windows Server 2019 / 2022. Direct downloads (.deb, .rpm, .msi, tar.gz) are also available — every file is GPG-signed.

### ASP.NET Core sidecar (NuGet)

mod_pagespeed {{V1_LINE}} also ships as a NuGet sidecar package, `WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar`, with native binaries delivered in the companion `WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar.NativeAssets.Linux` package. Linux-only (linux-x64 / linux-arm64); it bundles nginx {{SIDECAR_NGINX_VERSION}}.

It uses an inverse topology: your ASP.NET Core Kestrel application is the public front door, and the bundled nginx runs on loopback behind it as the optimize-proxy. Add it to your project with:

```
dotnet add package WeAmp.PageSpeed.Sidecar
```

The package is GA and indexed on nuget.org (no `--prerelease` flag needed). Defaults: `UseLaunchShim=true`, `OwnPublicPort=true`.

### Cyclone Cache

Replaces the open-source file-based cache:
- Fixed-size cache file — no runaway disk usage
- Lock-free reads — concurrent requests don't block each other
- Memory-mapped I/O — OS manages RAM caching
- Shared across processes — works with nginx workers and Apache prefork
- Also supports memcached and Redis as external cache backends

### Filters

44+ optimization filters organized in categories:

**CoreFilters (default):** add_head, combine_css, combine_javascript, convert_meta_tags, extend_cache, fallback_rewrite_css_urls, flatten_css_imports, inline_css, inline_import_to_link, inline_javascript, rewrite_css, rewrite_images, rewrite_javascript, rewrite_style_attributes_with_url

**Image filters:** rewrite_images (master), recompress_images/jpeg/png/webp, convert_jpeg_to_progressive, convert_jpeg_to_webp, convert_png_to_jpeg, convert_gif_to_png, convert_to_webp_lossless/animated, resize_images, responsive_images, lazyload_images, inline_images, sprite_images, strip_image_color_profile, strip_image_meta_data, plus the opt-in AVIF filters convert_jpeg_to_avif, convert_to_avif_lossless, convert_to_avif_animated, recompress_avif (enabled separately from rewrite_images)

**CSS filters:** rewrite_css, combine_css, inline_css, flatten_css_imports, prioritize_critical_css, move_css_above_scripts, move_css_to_head, inline_google_font_css

**JavaScript filters:** rewrite_javascript, combine_javascript, inline_javascript, defer_javascript, include_js_source_maps

**HTML filters:** collapse_whitespace, remove_comments, elide_attributes, trim_urls, insert_dns_prefetch, hint_preload_subresources

**Caching filters:** extend_cache (content-hashed URLs with 1-year TTL), extend_cache_pdfs, rewrite_domains

Three RewriteLevel presets: CoreFilters (default, production-safe), PassThrough (manual filter selection), OptimizeForBandwidth (in-place optimization only, no URL rewriting).

### Key Directives

- pagespeed on|off|standby|unplugged — module state
- pagespeed RewriteLevel CoreFilters|PassThrough|OptimizeForBandwidth
- pagespeed EnableFilters filter1,filter2 — add filters
- pagespeed DisableFilters filter1,filter2 — remove filters
- pagespeed ForbidFilters filter1,filter2 — lock out filters
- pagespeed Domain DOMAIN — authorize domain for fetching
- pagespeed MapOriginDomain FROM TO — map public domain to origin
- pagespeed MapRewriteDomain FROM TO — rewrite resource URLs
- pagespeed LoadFromFile URL PATH — load from filesystem
- pagespeed FetchHttps enable — enable HTTPS fetching
- pagespeed EnableCachePurge on — enable per-URL purge
- pagespeed InPlaceResourceOptimization on — optimize resources in-place (IPRO)

Apache uses ModPagespeed prefix (e.g., ModPagespeedEnableFilters). nginx uses pagespeed prefix with semicolon.

### Admin Pages

Built-in admin UI at /pagespeed_admin/:
- Statistics: filter activity, cache hit rates, latency
- Configuration: active filters, directive values
- Histograms: page load time, rewrite latency
- Caches: cache inspection, purge
- Console: historical graphs (requires StatisticsLogging)
- Messages: recent log messages

### Upgrading from mod_pagespeed {{V1_LINE}}

{{PRODUCT_NAME}} {{CURRENT_LINE}} is the drop-in upgrade for mod_pagespeed {{V1_LINE}} (same directives, same filters). The IIS package ships from the {{V1_LINE}} packaging channel. See [Upgrading to {{CURRENT_LINE}}](https://modpagespeed.com/1.1/docs/upgrading-to-2-1/).

Docker/Helm deployments on the ModPageSpeed {{V2_LINE}} engine move via the [migration guide](https://modpagespeed.com/docs/migrating-to-2-1/). The ASP.NET Core middleware continues unchanged — see [ASP.NET Core performance](https://modpagespeed.com/aspnet-core-performance/).

